<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/advanced/phenomenology-and-existentialism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Phenomenology and existentialism react against abstract systems by returning to
    <strong>lived experience — and the individual's situation within it</strong>. Phenomenology
    (Husserl, Merleau-Ponty) describes consciousness precisely; existentialism (Kierkegaard,
    Nietzsche, Sartre, de Beauvoir) asks how a free, finite being should live inside it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the method, the immersion, the freedom, the situation" widget="equationStep" config={phenCfg} />

  <h2>Why these schools matter outside philosophy departments</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>They shaped 20th-century culture:</strong> postwar literature (Camus, Beckett), psychiatry (existential therapy, Frankl's logotherapy from the camps), architecture (embodiment, place) and design's user-centred turn all carry their DNA.</li>
    <li><strong>Embodiment as insight:</strong> Merleau-Ponty argued the body is the vehicle of perception — decades before embodied-cognition research agreed. The mind is not a desktop; the phenomenologists noticed first.</li>
    <li><strong>Anxiety as information:</strong> existentialists read anxiety as the felt awareness of freedom and finitude — not a defect. Therapy streams (existential, ACT) turned that reading into treatment.</li>
    <li><strong>The critique that travels:</strong> bad faith, conformity and inauthenticity name recurring human strategies — the concepts are diagnostic tools for politics and organisations as much as for individuals.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Husserl (1900); Heidegger, Being and Time (1927); Sartre, Being and Nothingness (1943); de Beauvoir (1949); Frankl, Man's Search for Meaning (1946).</p>
</TopicPage>
